c08_draw_mucosa.text:
The **mucosa** region is drawn: you accepted it, and it holds 29,306 cells in one piece (annotation version 3).

**How I drew it:** the outline traces where four kinds of cells form tissue:
- epithelial cells;
- PDGFRA-high telocytes;
- plasma cells;
- cDC1 cells.

The last three stand for the lamina propria. I left out the GREM1+ fibroblasts and the T cells because many of them sit deep in the submucosa. 97% of those four kinds of cells fall inside the outline.

**What's inside:** 64% epithelial cells, 15% fibroblasts (mostly telocytes), 8% T cells, and smaller shares of endothelial, myeloid and mast cells.

**Against the H&E:** the outline covers the villi and crypts across the top of the section. Its lower edge runs along the muscle band under the mucosa, from rows F–G on the right down to rows J–K on the left.

**One edge to check:** at the bottom left (J1 to K5) the outline cuts through the lymphoid aggregate at the base of the mucosa, so its lower part is outside. That aggregate is part of the mucosa. You can extend the region over it with the Regions tools.

